STANCES=[
 (140, "NCCL algorithm and protocol selection, not the parallel axis itself, can dominate the observed tensor-parallel penalty at decode message sizes.",
  "At decode, the all-reduce payload per layer is hidden_size * dtype_bytes per token, which is small enough to land in NCCL's latency-bound regime. In that regime NCCL's choice of algorithm (ring versus tree versus NVLS) and protocol (LL, LL128, Simple) changes the latency floor by a large factor, and the choice is made by an internal tuning table keyed on message size, rank count and topology. A tensor-parallel arm that looks architecturally expensive may simply have fallen off the low-latency protocol because the tuner picked a bandwidth-optimal path for a message that is not bandwidth-bound.",
  "H140: pinning the collective to a latency-optimised algorithm and protocol will reduce decode all-reduce time at the tested tensor degree without changing any model-level setting, and the reduction will be larger at small tensor degree message sizes than at large prefill message sizes (ESTIMATE; derivation: LL and LL128 protocols trade payload efficiency for fewer synchronisation round-trips, which only pays when the message is below the protocol crossover, and decode payloads sit below prefill payloads by roughly the prompt-length factor).",
  "Hold model, tensor degree, batching policy and precision fixed and vary only the collective tuning: default tuner, forced latency protocol, forced tree algorithm. Capture an NCCL debug trace per arm confirming which algorithm and protocol were actually selected, rather than which was requested.",
  ["NCCL debug output naming the selected algorithm and protocol per message size, per arm",
   "Standalone all-reduce microbenchmark at the exact decode payload size, separate from the serving run",
   "Per-layer collective time from a device-side profile, with host time excluded",
   "Topology dump confirming the intra-node link type actually used between the participating ranks"],
  ["Forcing a protocol can help decode and hurt prefill in the same deployment, so a single global setting may be a regression somewhere",
   "Tuner behaviour is version-dependent, so a result is only valid for the exact library version recorded",
   "Microbenchmark numbers do not transfer to the serving path if the serving path overlaps collectives with compute"],
  0.58),
 (141, "KV-cache capacity, not step latency, is usually the real reason tensor parallelism wins on a latency SLO under concurrency.",
  "Tensor parallelism shards the KV cache along the head dimension, so per-GPU KV bytes fall roughly with the tensor degree. That raises the number of concurrent sequences that fit before the scheduler must preempt or queue. Queueing delay, once it appears, dominates any microsecond-scale collective cost, because a request waiting for a free cache block pays whole step times rather than fractions of one. So the honest causal story at realistic concurrency is often capacity relief, not faster arithmetic, and an experiment run at concurrency one will miss it entirely.",
  "H141: the latency advantage attributed to raising tensor degree will be near zero at concurrency one and will grow with offered concurrency, and the growth will track the point at which the lower tensor degree begins evicting or queueing for KV blocks (ESTIMATE; derivation: per-GPU KV bytes scale as total_kv_bytes / TP, so the admissible concurrency before cache exhaustion scales roughly linearly with TP, and queueing delay is zero until that ceiling is reached and then rises steeply).",
  "Sweep offered concurrency at fixed tensor degree, then repeat at a second tensor degree, holding batching policy, sequence-length distribution and precision fixed. Instrument cache occupancy and preemption or eviction counts alongside latency at every point.",
  ["KV-cache occupancy and free-block count sampled over the run at each concurrency point",
   "Preemption, eviction and queue-wait counters per arm, not just end-to-end latency",
   "TPOT p95 and TTFT p95 as functions of concurrency for both tensor degrees",
   "Sequence-length distribution of the offered load, since KV footprint is length-driven"],
  ["Concentrating on concurrency one produces a conclusion that inverts under production load",
   "Cache block size and allocation granularity differ across serving stacks, so the exhaustion point is stack-specific",
   "Long-tail sequence lengths can exhaust cache well before the mean-based capacity estimate predicts"],
  0.62),
 (142, "Pipeline parallelism only becomes defensible when it is the mechanism that keeps a stage off a slow interconnect, not when it is chosen for its own sake.",
  "Pipeline parallelism moves a small hidden-state tensor between adjacent stages once per micro-batch, whereas tensor parallelism moves an activation-sized tensor through a collective twice per layer. When the boundary being crossed is a slow link, that asymmetry is decisive: the point-to-point send survives a high-latency hop far better than a per-layer synchronous all-reduce does. The correct framing is therefore that pipeline parallelism is a tool for placing a cut on the cheapest available boundary, and its bubble cost is the price paid to avoid dragging collectives across that boundary.",
  "H142: when the deployment must span two nodes, placing the cut as a pipeline stage boundary will yield lower TPOT p95 than extending tensor parallelism across the same node boundary, and the margin will grow with inter-node link latency (ESTIMATE; derivation: pipeline crossing cost per token is (PP-1) point-to-point sends of hidden_size * dtype_bytes, while tensor crossing cost is 2 * L all-reduces over the same link, so the ratio of crossings is roughly 2L/(PP-1) in favour of the pipeline cut).",
  "Build two two-node arms that differ only in where the parallel cut falls: tensor parallelism spanning the node boundary, versus tensor parallelism confined within each node with a pipeline boundary between them. Hold total GPU count, model, precision and batching policy fixed.",
  ["Inter-node link type, negotiated rate and measured point-to-point latency at the relevant message size",
   "Per-token count and size of cross-node transfers for each arm, taken from a communication profile",
   "TPOT p95 and TTFT p95 per arm with variance across repeated trials",
   "Confirmation that intra-node topology is identical between the two arms"],
  ["A two-node result does not justify pipeline parallelism inside a single node, where the collective is cheap",
   "Pipeline bubble cost depends on the micro-batch count, so an unfair micro-batch setting can decide the comparison",
   "Cross-node fabric contention from unrelated traffic can masquerade as an architectural effect"],
  0.6),
 (143, "A latency-sensitive service must be evaluated at its admission-controlled operating point, or the parallelism comparison measures saturation rather than layout.",
  "Latency percentiles are only meaningful relative to a load level and an admission policy. Without a declared operating point, raising parallel degree can appear to improve latency merely because the arm with more capacity absorbs the same offered load further from saturation. Two layouts should be compared at matched utilisation, or at matched achieved throughput under a fixed latency bound, so that the reported percentile reflects the layout rather than the distance to the knee of the queueing curve.",
  "H143: comparing two parallel layouts at matched offered request rate will produce a different ranking than comparing them at matched achieved throughput under a fixed TPOT p95 bound, whenever the two layouts differ in admissible concurrency (ESTIMATE; derivation: queueing delay is a steep nonlinear function of utilisation, so at a fixed arrival rate the layout with greater capacity sits at lower utilisation and inherits a latency advantage that is a capacity effect rather than a per-step effect).",
  "For each layout, sweep offered load to build a full latency-versus-throughput curve rather than sampling a single point, then compare the layouts at the throughput each sustains under the same declared TPOT p95 bound. Keep admission and queueing policy identical across arms.",
  ["Full latency-versus-throughput curves per layout, not single operating points",
   "Declared admission policy, queue depth limit and rejection behaviour, identical across arms",
   "Achieved throughput at the fixed latency bound for each layout",
   "Utilisation or occupancy metric sampled during each load point"],
  ["Single-point comparisons are the most common way this decision is decided incorrectly",
   "Client-side load generators can themselves saturate and cap offered load, producing a false plateau",
   "Warm-up and cache-population effects distort early samples if the run is short"],
  0.63),
 (144, "Attention head count and grouped-query structure set a hard ceiling on usable tensor degree, and exceeding it converts sharding into replication.",
  "Tensor parallelism shards attention along heads. With grouped-query or multi-query attention the number of distinct key-value head groups is much smaller than the query head count, so once the tensor degree exceeds the key-value group count the key-value projections must be replicated across ranks rather than split. Past that point, additional tensor degree keeps adding collective cost while delivering no further reduction in key-value cache bytes per GPU, which is the main capacity benefit the sharding was bought for.",
  "H144: raising tensor degree beyond the model's key-value head-group count will leave per-GPU KV bytes unchanged while per-token collective time continues to rise, so admissible concurrency will plateau while TPOT p95 degrades (ESTIMATE; derivation: KV bytes per GPU scale as total_kv_bytes / min(TP, kv_groups), which stops falling once TP exceeds kv_groups, while the per-layer all-reduce count is unchanged and its latency is non-decreasing in rank count).",
  "Sweep tensor degree across values that straddle the model's key-value group count, holding everything else fixed, and instrument per-GPU key-value cache bytes directly rather than inferring them from total memory use.",
  ["Model configuration showing query head count, key-value head-group count and head dimension",
   "Measured per-GPU KV-cache bytes at each tensor degree, read from the serving stack rather than estimated",
   "Per-token collective time and TPOT p95 at each degree",
   "Admissible concurrency before cache exhaustion at each degree"],
  ["Assuming KV bytes always divide by tensor degree is wrong for grouped-query and multi-query models",
   "Some stacks silently pad or replicate rather than failing, so the regression appears only in latency and memory, not as an error",
   "Head-dimension padding at awkward tensor degrees can also reduce compute efficiency independently"],
  0.64),
 (145, "The cost of reconfiguration and failure recovery differs sharply between the two axes and belongs in the decision, not in a footnote.",
  "A tensor-parallel group fails as a unit: losing one rank invalidates every shard in the group, so recovery means restarting the whole group and reloading its weights. A pipeline stage failure is narrower in principle, since only that stage's layers must be restored, but in-flight micro-batches spanning the pipeline are lost and the stage's position in the chain must be repaired before service resumes. For a latency-sensitive service with an availability target, expected time-to-recovery multiplied by failure rate can outweigh a small steady-state latency difference, so the layout choice should be argued against the availability budget rather than against the p95 alone.",
  "H145: mean time to restore service after a single-GPU fault will differ measurably between the two layouts at equal total GPU count, and the difference will be dominated by the weight-reload volume each layout forces rather than by control-plane time (ESTIMATE; derivation: recovery time is approximately reload_bytes / effective_load_bandwidth plus fixed control-plane latency, and the reload volume is the whole tensor group's shards versus one pipeline stage's layers, which differ by roughly the stage count).",
  "Inject a single-rank fault under steady load in each layout and measure time from fault injection to restored steady-state latency, repeated enough times to report a distribution rather than one sample. Hold checkpoint format, storage path and load bandwidth fixed across arms.",
  ["Fault-injection procedure and timestamps for fault, detection, restart and recovery of steady-state latency",
   "Bytes reloaded per recovery event and the achieved load bandwidth",
   "Count of requests lost or errored per recovery event, per arm",
   "Availability budget the service is held to, stated before the test"],
  ["Recovery testing under synthetic load can understate the impact of a real fault during a traffic peak",
   "Control-plane timeouts may dominate on small models and hide the reload-volume effect",
   "Repeated fault injection can leave residual state that makes later trials unrepresentative"],
  0.55),
 (146, "Overlap of communication with computation, where the stack supports it, changes the tensor-parallel penalty from additive to partially hidden.",
  "The naive model of tensor-parallel cost treats every all-reduce as fully exposed on the critical path. Implementations that fuse or overlap the collective with adjacent computation, for example by chunking the down-projection and beginning the reduction on completed chunks, hide part of that latency behind work that would happen anyway. Whether this is available is an implementation property, not an architectural one, so the same tensor degree can look expensive or cheap depending on the kernel path taken. A comparison that does not record whether overlap was active is not reproducible.",
  "H146: with overlap enabled, the incremental TPOT cost per unit of tensor degree will be lower than with overlap disabled, and the gap will shrink as the collective becomes purely latency-bound at small payloads (ESTIMATE; derivation: overlap can hide at most the portion of collective time that is concurrent with available independent compute, and at very small payloads the collective is dominated by fixed synchronisation latency with little bandwidth term left to hide).",
  "Run the same tensor degree with the overlap path enabled and disabled, holding model, precision, batching and library version fixed, and confirm from a device timeline that the collective and compute kernels actually overlap rather than merely being permitted to.",
  ["Device-side kernel timeline showing collective and compute kernel concurrency per arm",
   "Exposed versus total collective time per step, separated",
   "Library and serving-stack versions with the overlap feature flag state recorded",
   "TPOT p95 per arm with repeated trials"],
  ["A feature flag being set does not prove the fast path was taken; the timeline must confirm it",
   "Overlap can increase memory pressure through extra buffers, which may reduce admissible concurrency",
   "Results are tightly version-coupled and expire when the stack is upgraded"],
  0.57),
 (147, "Sequence-length distribution, not the mean, decides which layout wins, because prefill and decode costs scale differently in length.",
  "Prefill cost grows superlinearly in prompt length through the attention term, while decode cost grows with generated length and with key-value cache size. A workload with a long tail of long prompts is prefill-heavy and rewards the layout that parallelises compute well; a workload of short prompts with long generations is decode-heavy and rewards the layout that minimises per-step collectives and maximises cache capacity. Reporting a single mean latency over a mixed distribution therefore blends two regimes that prefer opposite answers, and can produce a recommendation that is correct for neither.",
  "H147: segmenting the same benchmark run by prompt-length quantile will show the layout ranking inverting between the shortest and longest quantiles, even though the aggregate mean shows a single winner (ESTIMATE; derivation: TTFT is prefill-dominated and rises with prompt length while TPOT is decode-dominated and roughly length-independent per step, so the relative weight of the two terms shifts monotonically across length quantiles and the layouts optimise different terms).",
  "Replay one fixed, recorded request trace against both layouts and report latency percentiles segmented by prompt-length quantile and by output-length quantile, in addition to the aggregate. Do not resample the trace between arms.",
  ["The exact request trace used, with prompt and output length distributions, replayed identically across arms",
   "Latency percentiles segmented by length quantile, per arm",
   "Separate TTFT and TPOT reporting, never a blended latency",
   "Confirmation that the trace replay preserved arrival timing across arms"],
  ["Aggregate means hide regime inversion and are the usual source of an incorrect recommendation here",
   "Synthetic fixed-length workloads will not reproduce the effect at all",
   "Segmenting after the fact requires per-request length logging, which must be enabled before the run"],
  0.61),
 (148, "Memory fragmentation and allocator behaviour can dominate the capacity argument that the parallelism choice is supposed to settle.",
  "The capacity benefit claimed for higher tensor degree assumes freed memory is usable. In practice paged or block-based key-value allocators, activation workspaces and library-level caching allocators leave usable memory below the arithmetic remainder, and the shortfall varies with block size and request-length mix. A layout that theoretically admits more concurrency may not do so in a running process, and the gap appears as unexplained preemptions rather than as an allocation failure. The capacity claim must therefore be validated against observed admissible concurrency, not against a subtraction of weight bytes from device memory.",
  "H148: admissible concurrency measured on a running service will fall short of the value predicted by subtracting weight and workspace bytes from device memory, and the shortfall fraction will differ between layouts (ESTIMATE; derivation: usable cache capacity is floor(free_bytes / block_bytes) minus allocator overhead and reserved workspace, and both block granularity and workspace size differ with parallel degree, so the rounding and reservation losses do not scale identically).",
  "For each layout, compute the predicted admissible concurrency from configuration, then measure the concurrency at which preemption first occurs under a fixed length distribution, and report both numbers side by side rather than only the measured one.",
  ["Predicted versus measured admissible concurrency for each layout, with the prediction's arithmetic shown",
   "Allocator statistics including reserved, allocated and fragmented bytes sampled during the run",
   "Cache block size and workspace reservation per layout",
   "First-preemption concurrency under an identical length distribution across arms"],
  ["Estimating capacity from device memory minus weights systematically overstates it",
   "Allocator behaviour changes with library version and with environment tuning variables",
   "A long-running process can degrade over time, so a short test may not reveal fragmentation"],
  0.59),
 (149, "The decision should be recorded as a reversible configuration with a declared rollback gate, because the correct layout is workload-dependent and will drift.",
  "Every mechanism above is conditional on properties of the workload that change: length distribution, concurrency, acceptance rates under speculation, model revision. A layout chosen correctly today can become wrong after a prompt-template change or a traffic shift, with no code change and no alarm. The operationally safe posture is to treat parallel layout as a versioned, canaryable configuration with a pre-declared latency gate and an automatic revert, and to re-run the comparison on a schedule against a recent traffic trace rather than to treat the original benchmark as durable evidence.",
  "H149: re-running the identical layout comparison against a traffic trace captured at least one month later will change either the winning layout or the margin beyond the measured run-to-run variance, without any change to the serving stack (ESTIMATE; derivation: the ranking depends on prompt and output length distribution and offered concurrency, all of which are observed to drift with product usage, and the margins measured between layouts are typically small relative to that drift).",
  "Capture a dated traffic trace, run both layouts against it, record the result with the trace identifier, and repeat on a fixed schedule. Treat each result as valid only for its trace, and canary any layout change on a small traffic fraction with an automatic revert bound.",
  ["Dated, versioned traffic traces retained alongside each comparison result",
   "Canary configuration: traffic fraction, duration, and the exact latency and error bounds that trigger revert",
   "Run-to-run variance from repeated trials, so drift can be distinguished from noise",
   "An audit record linking each production layout to the trace and result that justified it"],
  ["Treating a one-off benchmark as durable evidence is the main long-run failure mode here",
   "Automatic revert can oscillate if the gate is tighter than measurement noise",
   "Traces containing user content require handling controls before being retained for replay"],
  0.56),
]
